6875, 0.01328277587890625, -0.037109375, -0.03680419921875, 0.00434112548828125, -0.01428985595703125, 0.002506256103515625, 0.03369140625, 0.044921875, 0.0265350341796875, 0.0247650146484375, 0.02532958984375, 0.0200347900390625, 0.0038700103759765625, -0.0303497314453125, 0.0014238357543945312, -0.006244659423828125, 0.016693115234375, -0.0171051025390625, -0.0123748779296875, 0.016082763671875, -0.0204315185546875, -0.0369873046875, -0.0003032684326171875, -0.01409912109375, 0.0055084228515625, -0.0021381378173828125, -0.0182342529296875, -0.0282745361328125, 0.0106048583984375, -0.008087158203125, -0.018890380859375, 0.03717041015625, -0.01474761962890625, 0.026275634765625, 0.00901031494140625, -0.04473876953125, -0.0298004150390625, 0.004974365234375, 0.02069091796875, -0.02874755859375, 0.0003006458282470703, 0.0266265869140625, -0.0035381317138671875, -0.0096893310546875, -0.020416259765625, -0.010833740234375, 0.04156494140625, -0.01422882080078125, 0.0252838134765625, 0.006778717041015625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "# Introduction\n\nGeometric Algebra (GA) is a mathematical framework that extends traditional vector algebra by introducing new elements called multivectors, which include scalars, vectors, bivectors (representing oriented planes), trivectors, and higher-grade elements. GA provides a unified language for representing geometric transformations and relationships, allowing for more natural and compact descriptions of complex systems in mathematics, physics, and engineering. Its algebraic structure encompasses both commutative and non-commutative operations, making it particularly powerful for modeling rotations, reflections, and other geometric operations.\n\n# Background\n\nRecent publications have explored the application of GA to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, especially in the context of electrical circuits and mechanical systems [2,4]. A key aspect of system analysis is the transformation of ordinary differential equations (ODEs) from the real domain to the Laplace domain. The Laplace transform is a fundamental tool in engineering and physics for converting time-domain differential equations into algebraic equations in the frequency domain, simplifying the analysis of linear systems and enabling the use of algebraic techniques for system design and stability analysis.\n\nTraditional Laplace transforms, however, are defined for real- or complex-valued functions and do not directly apply to multivector-valued functions or the algebraic structures present in GA. While extensions of the Laplace transform to hyper-complex algebras such as quaternions and Clifford algebras have been proposed [1,3], these approaches often face challenges in handling the full range of multivector elements and the non-commutative properties inherent in GA.\n\n# Proposed Method\n\nIn this work, we introduce a definition of the Laplace transform specifically tailored for Geometric Algebra, building upon earlier efforts in the literature [1,3]. Our approach is applicable to geometric algebras with signature (p, q) where the sum p + q \u2264 5. This limitation is due to the exponential growth in the number of basis elements (2^(p+q)) and the increasing complexity of multivector multiplication as the dimension increases. For p + q > 5, the explicit construction and manipulation of the Laplace transform become computationally intensive, and the non-commutative multiplication of higher-grade elements introduces additional algebraic challenges that are not easily addressed with current computational tools.\n\nOur method systematically extends the Laplace transform to multivector-valued functions by defining the transform component-wise for each grade and ensuring that the algebraic structure of GA is preserved. Special attention is given to the non-commutative nature of multivector multiplication, particularly when dealing with products of vectors and higher-grade elements. The proposed definition ensures that the order of multiplication is maintained throughout the transformation process, which is essential for the correct representation of geometric relationships.\n\n# Key Properties\n\nThe proposed Laplace transform in GA retains key properties such as linearity: the transform of a sum of multivector-valued functions equals the sum of their transforms. It also accommodates the transformation of multivector-valued functions, allowing for the direct handling of quantities like bivectors and trivectors that naturally arise in GA-based models. By preserving the geometric structure of the problem, the transform enables the analysis of systems where voltages, currents, or other physical quantities are represented as multivectors.\n\nCompared to previous approaches [1,3], our method extends the applicability of the Laplace transform to a broader class of multivector-valued functions and provides a systematic framework for its use within GA. Unlike earlier definitions that may be limited to specific grades or commutative subalgebras, our approach addresses the challenges of non-commutativity and the handling of higher-grade elements, making it more suitable for practical engineering applications.\n\n# Applications\n\nPotential applications of the proposed Laplace transform include the analysis of RLC circuits where voltages and currents are modeled as multivectors, enabling the simultaneous treatment of scalar and vector components. For example, in a three-phase electrical circuit, each phase can be represented as a vector, and the combined system as a multivector. The Laplace transform allows the time-domain circuit equations to be converted into the frequency domain while preserving the geometric relationships between phases. Another example is the study of mechanical systems with rotational dynamics, where angular velocities and torques are naturally described by bivectors. The proposed transform simplifies the analysis and control of such systems by enabling direct manipulation of their multivector representations.\n\n# Comparison with Existing Methods\n\nExisting methods for extending the Laplace transform to hyper-complex algebras, such as those described by Adler [1] and Hitzer [3], often focus on specific algebraic structures like quaternions or Clifford algebras and may not generalize to the full range of multivector elements in GA. These approaches can be limited by assumptions of commutativity or by restricting the transform to certain grades. Our method differs by providing a general framework that systematically handles all grades and explicitly addresses the non-commutative multiplication rules of GA, thereby broadening the scope of systems that can be analyzed using the Laplace transform.\n\n# References\n\n[1] S. L. Adler, \"Quaternionic Quantum Mechanics and Quantum Fields,\" Oxford University Press, 1995.  \n[2] D. Hestenes and G. Sobczyk, \"Clifford Algebra to Geometric Calculus: A Unified Language for Mathematics and Physics,\" Reidel, 1984.  \n[3] E. Hitzer, \"Quaternion and Clifford Fourier and Laplace Transforms,\" Advances in Applied Clifford Algebras, vol. 17, no. 3, pp. 497\u2013517, 2007.  \n[4] A. Lasenby, J. Lasenby, and R. Wareham, \"A Covariant Approach to Geometry Using Geometric Algebra,\" Acta Applicandae Mathematicae, vol. 110, no. 2, pp. 185\u2013233, 2010."}}